					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mortos" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1705184); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1705184&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606466833" alt="Mortos" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1705184)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mortos" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1705184); return false;" >Mortos</a></span> <span class="odate time_1527861703 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Jun 2018 14:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3820102">
									

<p>There's some interesting stuff here but you have a lot of moving parts that don't necessarily work together as well as they could. Just to try and list everything going on here:<br />
- Anomalous plants containing a substance similar to brain matter that attracts lizards, whose growth and health is tied to the relative success of the institution they are planted by.<br />
- A colossal tree thats 30 times larger than the biggest tree on earth and yet somehow hasn't been noticed by anyone (a fact that you only briefly touch upon).<br />
- A portal underneath this tree to an alternate dimension of some sort that contains more of these plants.<br />
- A bunch of other trees that don't attract lizards for some reason, except for a single 250m long lizard which eats one of the trees, and when these trees are damaged incredibly precisely targeted portions of the world seem to lose specific pieces of knowledge.</p>
<p>Any one of these points could make an entire article by themselves. This isn't necessarily a <em>bad</em> thing, I just wanted to point out how much was going on here.</p>
<p>The problem with it is, the entire thing seems to exist to lead to that giant tree with its portal and anomalous valley place. I'm not sure the article would actually lose anything if everything besides those points were removed. On top of that you've got a lot of very loose and nonclinical language and awkward phrasing through the article that could have been cleaned up with a bit more editing and feedback (I see you only got feedback from 3 people, which feels insufficient for an article this busy).</p>
<p>Anyway the implications are interesting - that these trees are somehow tied to human knowledge is what I'm taking away from it, and that the destruction of these trees removes that knowledge? And therefore suggesting that the presence of the already-destroyed tree means we're already missing something, possibly something big.</p>
<p>No vote from me, but you do have some interesting hooks here.</p>

